// Target-neutral greedy register allocator (GPR-only, cross-block via
// spill-at-boundaries).
//
// PHASE 1 + PHASE 2 of the greedy-allocator port. This is the foundational scaffold for an
// alternative to the SSA-coloring pipeline (colorSSA -> Belady -> reactive spill
// loop -> applyColoring). That pipeline hangs non-deterministically on a few very
// dense functions during self-compile (unbounded op emission in applyColoring),
// so a few functions are better served by a simpler allocator whose work is
// bounded by op count.
//
// The algorithm is ported from maxon-sharp's RegisterManagerBase: a register file
// (registerContents / valueToRegister / lastUsed / stackHome / constants) driven
// by a single forward walk over the function's ops. For each op it ensures every
// read operand is resident (reloading or rematerializing as needed), frees reads
// that die at the op, then allocates a register for each def (evicting the LRU
// occupant on pressure). Unlike the C# version — which allocates DURING lowering
// and emits physical-register ops inline — this runs as a PASS over MIR that
// already carries virtual registers, so it must emit the FINAL PHYSICAL MIR
// itself, bypassing ColoringResult + applyColoring.
//
// Direct physical emission reuses the EXISTING per-op substitution machinery: the
// greedy walk builds a per-op ColoringResult (vreg -> the physical register it
// chose for THIS op) and hands it to regTarget.substituteOp — the same call
// applyColoring makes. Spill / reload / rematerialize ops are emitted through the
// same RegAllocTarget.emitSpill / emitReload / emitMovImm primitives the SSA
// spill path uses. No new target interface is introduced: RegAllocTarget +
// TargetOpQuery already expose every emit and analysis hook the greedy allocator
// needs.
//
// CROSS-BLOCK MODEL (Phase 2 — spill-at-boundaries, correctness-first):
//   * Blocks are processed in layout order. At each block START the register
//     file is EMPTY — every value live INTO a block lives in its stack home and
//     is reloaded on first use (greedyEnsureInReg reloads from the home).
//   * At each block EXIT (before the terminator) every value live-OUT of the
//     block that is still resident is spilled to its stack home so successors
//     can reload it. Values evicted mid-block are already in their home; constant
//     values are rematerialized in the successor and never spilled.
//   * BLOCK-ARGS / PHI: a branch passes block-args (the source vreg) to a
//     successor's block-params (a different vreg — the SSA edge rename). Greedy
//     routes the rename through the PARAM's stack home: at the predecessor exit
//     each arg's value is stored into its successor-param's home, and the
//     successor body reloads the param from that home. The arg SOURCES are read
//     into registers FIRST, on the path SHARED by all the block's out-edges, so a
//     self-referential phi permutation (arg == another param of the same edge,
//     e.g. a loop body that swaps its carried values) reads every source before
//     any store overwrites a home. The param-home STORES are then placed on each
//     edge's OWN dynamic path — exactly like destroySSA's critical-edge splitting:
//     the fallthrough edge's stores go inline after the branch, the taken edge's
//     stores go in a freshly-inserted trampoline block. This is REQUIRED, not an
//     optimization: a param's home double-serves as the current live value (for a
//     sibling edge whose successor reads the param's PRE-iteration value) AND as
//     the next-iteration phi destination (the back-edge), distinguishable only by
//     which edge is dynamically taken. Storing unconditionally before the branch
//     would let a loop latch's back-edge clobber the home a side-exit still needs.
//   * To make reload offsets valid regardless of block processing order (a loop
//     header reloads a param whose home a not-yet-processed latch writes), every
//     GLOBAL value — block params plus every value in any block's live-out set —
//     is assigned a stack home UP FRONT, in numeric value-id order (deterministic).
//   * Spill/reload ops emitted between a flag-setting compare and a flag-reading
//     conditional branch are flag-neutral on both targets (x64 mov / arm64
//     ldr/str). The one exception is constant REMATERIALIZATION: x64's value-0
//     `xor reg, reg` zeroing idiom CLEARS the flags, so an edge-arg source that is
//     a rematerialized constant must use the flag-preserving emit at a conditional
//     boundary — greedyReadEdgeArgs passes flagSafe there so the materialization
//     never clobbers the branch condition (arm64's movz is already flag-safe).
//
// CALL MODEL (Phase 3 — direct calls, GPR-only):
//   A call lowers (pre-regalloc) to a run of arg-setup movs (`mov physArgReg,
//   virtual(srcId)` plus stack-overflow stores), the call op, then post-call
//   result-capture movs (`mov virtual(resultId), physReturnReg`). The greedy walk
//   recognizes these regions exactly as ApplyColoring's walkBlockWithCallArgBuffer
//   does, and REUSES the same parallel-copy sequencer:
//     * Before the call, every live-across value resident in a register the call
//       destroys (caller-saved GPRs, plus any callee-saved reg used as an arg slot
//       — e.g. x64's rbx 7th-arg) is spilled to its stack home so it survives
//       (mirrors RegisterManagerBase.SpillAllLiveRegisters). Values dead at the
//       call (consumed args) are skipped.
//     * Arg sources are made resident greedily and their chosen registers recorded
//       in the per-op coloring, so classifyCallArgSetup resolves each move to a
//       physical ordinal; the buffered moves go through sequentializeCallArgSetup,
//       which breaks source->dest cycles (xchg / scratch) — the reason a value that
//       is another arg's destination is placed correctly.
//     * The call op is substituted, then the destroyed registers are invalidated in
//       the register file (mirrors InvalidateCallerSavedRegisters): their pre-call
//       values are in homes or dead, so later uses reload.
//     * Result/error-flag captures allocate the dest vregs greedily and run through
//       sequentializeCallResultCapture, which breaks the swap/chain the two return
//       registers can form. Callee-saved-resident values survive the call untouched
//       (no spill, no invalidate).
//
// SCOPE / DEFERRALS:
//   * GPR-only. The FP register file is a later phase: any FP-pattern op or FP
//     block-arg routed here panics with a clear message rather than miscompiling.
//   * DIRECT calls only. An indirect/witness call (the callee or fn-ptr arrives in
//     a virtual register, possibly via an interleaved load) panics — pinning that
//     operand outside the arg/clobber set is a later phase.
//   * Implicit-GPR-clobber ops (idiv/cqo and friends, which write a FIXED register
//     the allocator does not model) panic — also a later phase (P4).
//   * Routing is a forced test trigger (--greedy-only=NAME); the real
//     budget-then-greedy fallback trigger comes later. The greedy path therefore
//     never fires during normal compilation.

// ============================================================
// Forced-route configuration (test trigger)
// ============================================================

// Module-level config holder. Maxon does not allow a module-level `var` to be
// initialized with a String literal directly, so the name lives inside a holder
// type (the same pattern as SSAColoring's diagContext).
type GreedyConfig
	export var onlyFuncName as String

	export static function create() returns GreedyConfig
		return Self{onlyFuncName: ""}
	end 'create'
end 'GreedyConfig'

var greedyConfig = GreedyConfig.create()

// Set the single function name the greedy path should claim (from
// `--greedy-only=NAME`). Empty disables the greedy path entirely.
export function setGreedyOnlyFunc(name String)
	greedyConfig.onlyFuncName = name
end 'setGreedyOnlyFunc'

// True iff `funcName` is the force-routed greedy function. Always false when no
// `--greedy-only` was given, so normal compilation never takes the greedy path.
export function greedyOnlyFuncMatches(funcName String) returns bool
	if greedyConfig.onlyFuncName.isEmpty() 'noTrigger'
		return false
	end 'noTrigger'
	return funcName == greedyConfig.onlyFuncName
end 'greedyOnlyFuncMatches'

// ============================================================
// Mask helpers (a small set of register ordinals, as a bitset)
// ============================================================

// Protect masks track the physical registers that must NOT be evicted while
// allocating the current op (its still-live source operands). Ordinals are small
// (<= 31 on every supported target) so a plain RegInt bitset suffices.
function greedyMaskSet(mask RegInt, ord RegOrdinal) returns RegInt
	if ord < 0 'invalid'
		return mask
	end 'invalid'
	return mask or (1 shl ord)
end 'greedyMaskSet'

function greedyMaskHas(mask RegInt, ord RegOrdinal) returns bool
	if ord < 0 'invalid'
		return false
	end 'invalid'
	return ((mask shr ord) and 1) != 0
end 'greedyMaskHas'

// ============================================================
// Op-class guard
// ============================================================

// True for OpPattern values that touch the FP register file. The greedy
// allocator is GPR-only this phase; an FP op must not be silently treated as GPR.
// The FP-class patterns are contiguous in the OpPattern declaration
// (fpSingleDef..baseUseFpDef), so the surrounding ranges are pure GPR/control.
function greedyIsFpPattern(pat OpPattern) returns bool
	return match pat 'check'
		passThrough to baseUseDest gives false
		fpSingleDef to baseUseFpDef gives true
		movRegReg to fourAddr gives false
	end 'check'
end 'greedyIsFpPattern'

// Panic loudly when a force-routed function contains an op the greedy allocator
// cannot handle yet (an FP op or an implicit-GPR-clobber op). Misrouting is a
// test/dev error, so a clear panic beats a silent miscompile. Later phases
// replace each panic with real handling.
//
// CALL ops are NOT guarded here — Phase 3 handles direct calls in the dedicated
// call-region path (greedyProcessCall), and they legitimately carry implicit
// caller-saved clobbers, so the implicit-clobber guard below must skip them.
function greedyGuardOpSupported(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, opQuery TargetOpQuery, module TargetModule, packed OpIndex)
	if opQuery.isCallOp(module, packed: packed) 'isCall'
		return
	end 'isCall'

	let patOrd = opQuery.getOpPatternOrdinal(module, packed: packed)
	let pat = try OpPattern.fromRawValue(patOrd) otherwise OpPattern.passThrough
	if greedyIsFpPattern(pat) 'isFp'
		panic("greedy: FP op (pattern {pat.name}) in func `{state.funcName}` is not supported yet (the FP register file is deferred to a later phase) — do not route functions with floating-point via --greedy-only")
	end 'isFp'

	// Implicit-GPR-clobber ops (x64 idiv/cqo write RAX:RDX; widening integer
	// multiply writes the hi:lo pair) destroy a FIXED register the greedy
	// allocator does not yet model. A value the allocator believes survives the op
	// would be silently clobbered. Modeling these fixed clobbers is a later phase
	// (P4); until then, refuse rather than miscompile. Calls are excluded above —
	// their caller-saved clobbers ARE modeled (greedyProcessCall).
	if regTarget.getImplicitGprDefs(module, packed: packed) != 0 'implicitClobber'
		panic("greedy: op with an implicit GPR clobber (e.g. x64 idiv/cqo) in func `{state.funcName}` is not supported yet (fixed-register clobbers are modeled in a later phase) — do not route functions with integer division/modulo via --greedy-only")
	end 'implicitClobber'
end 'greedyGuardOpSupported'

// ============================================================
// Register-file primitives (ported from RegisterManagerBase)
// ============================================================

// True iff physical register `ord` currently holds no value.
function greedyRegisterIsFree(state RegState, ord RegOrdinal) returns bool
	return getOr(state.registerContents, index: ord, fallback: RM_NONE) == RM_NONE
end 'greedyRegisterIsFree'

// Bind value `vid` to physical register `ord`. Clears any stale mapping from a
// register the value previously occupied (mirrors RegisterManagerBase.Assign).
function greedyAssign(state RegState, ord RegOrdinal, vid RegInt)
	var info = getValueInfo(state, valueId: vid)
	let oldReg = info.gprOrdinal
	if oldReg != RM_NONE and oldReg != ord 'clearOld'
		let oldOccupant = getOr(state.registerContents, index: oldReg, fallback: RM_NONE)
		if oldOccupant == vid 'stillOurs'
			set(state.registerContents, index: oldReg, value: RM_NONE, sentinel: RM_NONE)
			set(state.lastUsed, index: oldReg, value: 0, sentinel: 0)
		end 'stillOurs'
	end 'clearOld'

	set(state.registerContents, index: ord, value: vid, sentinel: RM_NONE)
	info.gprOrdinal = ord
	set(state.lastUsed, index: ord, value: state.currentOpIndex, sentinel: 0)
end 'greedyAssign'

// Record that `vid` is an integer constant `value`, so a later reload can
// rematerialize it (movImm) instead of round-tripping through a stack slot, and
// so eviction need not spill it at all (mirrors RegisterManagerBase constants).
function greedyRecordConstant(state RegState, vid RegInt, value RegInt)
	var info = getValueInfo(state, valueId: vid)
	info.constantValue = value
	info.isConstant = true
end 'greedyRecordConstant'

// Allocate the next spill-slot offset, respecting the target's stack-growth
// direction. Matches RegState's spill-offset convention so the spill/reload ops
// reference the same [fp/rbp + offset] slots the prologue/epilogue pass reserves
// for via func.spillSize.
function greedyAllocateSpillSlot(state RegState) returns RegInt
	if state.desc.spillsGrowDown 'growDown'
		state.nextSpillOffset = state.nextSpillOffset - 8
		return state.nextSpillOffset
	end 'growDown' else 'growUp'
		let slot = state.nextSpillOffset
		state.nextSpillOffset = state.nextSpillOffset + 8
		return slot
	end 'growUp'
end 'greedyAllocateSpillSlot'

// Find the least-recently-used evictable register, optionally constrained to
// registers whose occupant is a constant (preferred — free to rematerialize) or
// whose home is already in-sync (preferred next — eviction needs no fresh spill
// store). Returns RM_NONE if no register matches. Mirrors
// RegisterManagerBase.FindLruRegister.
//
// `requireInSync` keys on `homeInSync`, NOT merely on `stackHome` being
// reserved: greedyPreassignHomes gives every cross-block value a `stackHome` up
// front, so a reserved-but-unpopulated home does NOT mean the slot holds the
// value. Preferring such a victim and skipping its store (the old behavior)
// left the home holding garbage that the successor then reloaded.
function greedyFindLruRegister(state RegState, protectMask RegInt, requireConstant bool, requireInSync bool) returns RegOrdinal
	var bestReg = RM_NONE
	var bestLastUsed = i64.max
	for ord in state.desc.gprPool 'eachReg'
		let occupant = getOr(state.registerContents, index: ord, fallback: RM_NONE)
		if occupant == RM_NONE 'free'
			continue
		end 'free'
		if greedyMaskHas(protectMask, ord: ord) 'protected'
			continue
		end 'protected'

		let info = getValueInfo(state, valueId: occupant)
		if requireConstant and not info.isConstant 'notConst'
			continue
		end 'notConst'
		if requireInSync and not info.homeInSync 'notInSync'
			continue
		end 'notInSync'

		let lu = getOr(state.lastUsed, index: ord, fallback: 0)
		if lu < bestLastUsed 'better'
			bestLastUsed = lu
			bestReg = ord
		end 'better'
	end 'eachReg'
	return bestReg
end 'greedyFindLruRegister'

// Free physical register `ord`, spilling its occupant to its stack home first
// unless the value is rematerializable (constant) or its home is already
// in-sync (a prior store populated it and the SSA value has not changed since).
// Mirrors RegisterManagerBase.SpillRegisterIfOccupied.
//
// A reserved-but-not-yet-in-sync home is the common case here: every
// cross-block value gets a `stackHome` from greedyPreassignHomes but no store,
// so the FIRST eviction must actually emit the spill (and mark the home
// in-sync). Skipping it because a home was merely reserved is the garbage-spill
// bug this decoupling fixes.
function greedySpillRegisterIfOccupied(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, ord RegOrdinal)
	let occupant = getOr(state.registerContents, index: ord, fallback: RM_NONE)
	if occupant == RM_NONE 'empty'
		return
	end 'empty'

	var info = getValueInfo(state, valueId: occupant)
	if not info.isConstant and not info.homeInSync 'needsSpill'
		if info.stackHome == RM_NO_STACK_HOME 'allocHome'
			info.stackHome = greedyAllocateSpillSlot(state)
		end 'allocHome'
		regTarget.emitSpill(module, dstBlock: block, regOrdinal: ord, stackOffset: info.stackHome)
		info.homeInSync = true
	end 'needsSpill'

	info.gprOrdinal = RM_NONE
	set(state.registerContents, index: ord, value: RM_NONE, sentinel: RM_NONE)
	set(state.lastUsed, index: ord, value: 0, sentinel: 0)
end 'greedySpillRegisterIfOccupied'

// Evict one register so a new value can be placed. Prefers evicting a constant
// (no spill), then a value whose home is already in-sync (no fresh spill
// store), then any value (greedySpillRegisterIfOccupied emits the store).
// Mirrors RegisterManagerBase.Evict.
function greedyEvict(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, protectMask RegInt)
	var victim = greedyFindLruRegister(state, protectMask: protectMask, requireConstant: true, requireInSync: false)
	if victim == RM_NONE 'tryInSync'
		victim = greedyFindLruRegister(state, protectMask: protectMask, requireConstant: false, requireInSync: true)
	end 'tryInSync'
	if victim == RM_NONE 'tryAny'
		victim = greedyFindLruRegister(state, protectMask: protectMask, requireConstant: false, requireInSync: false)
	end 'tryAny'

	if victim == RM_NONE 'noVictim'
		panic("greedy: no register available to evict in func `{state.funcName}` (every pooled register is protected for the current op — register demand exceeds the pool)")
	end 'noVictim'

	greedySpillRegisterIfOccupied(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, ord: victim)
end 'greedyEvict'

// Allocate a physical register for value `vid`. Tries the next fresh pool slot,
// then any free register, then evicts the LRU occupant. `protectMask` names
// registers that must survive (the current op's still-live source operands).
// Mirrors RegisterManagerBase.AllocateRegister.
function greedyAllocateReg(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, vid RegInt, protectMask RegInt) returns RegOrdinal
	// 1. Next sequential fresh slot.
	if state.nextFreshIndex < state.desc.gprPool.count() 'tryFresh'
		let cand = try state.desc.gprPool.get(state.nextFreshIndex) otherwise panic("greedy: gprPool.get OOB at nextFreshIndex={state.nextFreshIndex}")
		if greedyRegisterIsFree(state, ord: cand) and not greedyMaskHas(protectMask, ord: cand) 'freshUsable'
			state.nextFreshIndex = state.nextFreshIndex + 1
			greedyAssign(state, ord: cand, vid: vid)
			return cand
		end 'freshUsable'
	end 'tryFresh'

	// 2. Any free register in pool order.
	for ord in state.desc.gprPool 'anyFree'
		if greedyRegisterIsFree(state, ord: ord) and not greedyMaskHas(protectMask, ord: ord) 'usable'
			greedyAssign(state, ord: ord, vid: vid)
			return ord
		end 'usable'
	end 'anyFree'

	// 3. All occupied — evict the LRU, then take the freed register.
	greedyEvict(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, protectMask: protectMask)
	for ord in state.desc.gprPool 'afterEvict'
		if greedyRegisterIsFree(state, ord: ord) 'nowFree'
			greedyAssign(state, ord: ord, vid: vid)
			return ord
		end 'nowFree'
	end 'afterEvict'

	panic("greedy: failed to allocate a register for v{vid} in func `{state.funcName}` after eviction")
end 'greedyAllocateReg'

// Ensure value `vid` is resident in a physical register, reloading from its
// stack home or rematerializing a constant as needed. Returns the holding
// ordinal. Mirrors RegisterManagerBase.EnsureInRegister.
//
// `flagSafe` (default false) forbids the flag-clobbering value-0 zeroing idiom
// when rematerializing a constant: it routes the materialization through
// emitMovImmFlagSafe instead of emitMovImm. Callers that emit between a block's
// flag-setting compare and its flag-reading conditional branch (the edge-arg
// source reads at a conditional boundary) pass true; everywhere else the cheaper
// emitMovImm is used. Reloads and evictions are flag-neutral on every target, so
// only the constant-remat path is affected.
function greedyEnsureInReg(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, vid RegInt, protectMask RegInt, flagSafe bool = false) returns RegOrdinal
	let info = getValueInfo(state, valueId: vid)

	if info.gprOrdinal != RM_NONE 'resident'
		set(state.lastUsed, index: info.gprOrdinal, value: state.currentOpIndex, sentinel: 0)
		return info.gprOrdinal
	end 'resident'

	if info.isConstant 'remat'
		let reg = greedyAllocateReg(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, vid: vid, protectMask: protectMask)
		if flagSafe 'flagSafeRemat'
			regTarget.emitMovImmFlagSafe(module, dstBlock: block, regOrdinal: reg, value: info.constantValue)
		end 'flagSafeRemat' else 'plainRemat'
			regTarget.emitMovImm(module, dstBlock: block, regOrdinal: reg, value: info.constantValue)
		end 'plainRemat'
		return reg
	end 'remat'

	if info.stackHome != RM_NO_STACK_HOME 'reload'
		let reg = greedyAllocateReg(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, vid: vid, protectMask: protectMask)
		regTarget.emitReload(module, dstBlock: block, regOrdinal: reg, stackOffset: info.stackHome)
		return reg
	end 'reload'

	panic("greedy: value v{vid} in func `{state.funcName}` has no register, no stack home, and is not a constant — it is read before any def or boundary store reached it. Every cross-block (global) value is pre-assigned a stack home, so this means a value was read in a block that neither defines it nor receives it as a live-in (a malformed CFG / liveness mismatch).")
end 'greedyEnsureInReg'

// Mark `vid` dead: release its register. Mirrors RegisterManagerBase.NoteValueDead
// (GPR side only — FP is a later phase).
function greedyNoteValueDead(state RegState, vid RegInt)
	var info = getValueInfo(state, valueId: vid)
	let ord = info.gprOrdinal
	if ord != RM_NONE 'inReg'
		info.gprOrdinal = RM_NONE
		set(state.registerContents, index: ord, value: RM_NONE, sentinel: RM_NONE)
		set(state.lastUsed, index: ord, value: 0, sentinel: 0)
	end 'inReg'
end 'greedyNoteValueDead'

// ============================================================
// Per-block last-use scan
// ============================================================

// Result of the pre-walk: per-value last-use op index (RM_NONE = never read) and
// the maximum value id referenced (for sizing the per-op coloring + scratch).
type GreedyLastUse
	export var lastUse as RegIntArray
	export var maxValueId as RegInt

	export static function create(lastUse RegIntArray, maxValueId RegInt) returns GreedyLastUse
		return Self{lastUse: lastUse, maxValueId: maxValueId}
	end 'create'
end 'GreedyLastUse'

// Scan the block (regular ops then terminator) recording, per read value, the
// index of its last reading op. The terminator's reads are attributed to index
// `block.opRefs.count()` — one past the last regular op — matching the op index
// the driver uses when it processes the terminator.
function greedyBuildLastUse(module TargetModule, block IrBlock, regTarget RegAllocTarget) returns GreedyLastUse
	var lastUse = RegIntArray.create()
	var maxValueId = 0
	var opIdx = 0

	for packed in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
		let reads = RegIntArray.create()
		regTarget.countOpReads(module, reads: reads, packed: packed)
		for r in reads 'eachRead'
			if r > maxValueId 'biggerR'
				maxValueId = r
			end 'biggerR'
			set(lastUse, index: r, value: opIdx, sentinel: RM_NONE)
		end 'eachRead'

		let defs = RegIntArray.create()
		regTarget.noteOpDefs(module, defs: defs, packed: packed)
		for d in defs 'eachDef'
			if d > maxValueId 'biggerD'
				maxValueId = d
			end 'biggerD'
		end 'eachDef'

		opIdx = opIdx + 1
	end 'eachOp'

	if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
		let treads = RegIntArray.create()
		regTarget.countOpReads(module, reads: treads, packed: block.terminatorIndex())
		for r in treads 'eachTermRead'
			if r > maxValueId 'biggerT'
				maxValueId = r
			end 'biggerT'
			set(lastUse, index: r, value: opIdx, sentinel: RM_NONE)
		end 'eachTermRead'
	end 'hasTerm'

	// Block-arg (phi) SOURCE vregs carried on outgoing edges are read at the block
	// BOUNDARY (greedyReadEdgeArgs), at the same index the terminator uses — one
	// past the last regular op. An edge-arg source is NOT in dataflow liveOut
	// (liveness attributes it to the successor's renamed PARAM, not to the source),
	// so without recording its boundary use here greedyProcessOp's freeDead would
	// free it at its last REGULAR read and the boundary edge copy would then find it
	// with no register, no home, and not constant — a panic. Recording it as a
	// boundary use keeps it resident (or spilled with a home) until the edge copy.
	// Mirrors LiveRangeBuilder's recordArgIdsLastUses.
	for edge in block.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
		for argId in edge.argIds 'eachArg'
			if argId > maxValueId 'biggerArg'
				maxValueId = argId
			end 'biggerArg'
			set(lastUse, index: argId, value: opIdx, sentinel: RM_NONE)
		end 'eachArg'
	end 'eachEdge'

	return GreedyLastUse.create(lastUse, maxValueId: maxValueId)
end 'greedyBuildLastUse'

function greedyIsLastUse(lastUse RegIntArray, vid RegInt, opIdx RegInt) returns bool
	return getOr(lastUse, index: vid, fallback: RM_NONE) == opIdx
end 'greedyIsLastUse'

// ============================================================
// Per-op driver
// ============================================================

// Resize `arr` to exactly `n` entries holding `fill` (used to pre-size the
// per-op coloring + constant-detection scratch so index writes are in bounds).
function greedyMakeIntArray(n RegInt, fill RegInt) returns RegIntArray
	var arr = RegIntArray.create()
	var i = 0
	while i < n 'init'
		arr.push(fill)
		i = i + 1
	end 'init'
	return arr
end 'greedyMakeIntArray'

function greedyMakeBoolArray(n RegInt) returns BoolArray
	var arr = BoolArray.create()
	var i = 0
	while i < n 'init'
		arr.push(false)
		i = i + 1
	end 'init'
	return arr
end 'greedyMakeBoolArray'

// If `packed` defines `valueId` with an integer constant, record it for
// rematerialization. Uses the target's existing constant-detection query.
function greedyMaybeRecordConstant(state RegState, opQuery TargetOpQuery, module TargetModule, packed OpIndex, valueId RegInt, isConstScratch BoolArray, constValScratch RegIntArray)
	opQuery.detectConstant(module, packed: packed, valueId: valueId, isConst: isConstScratch, constVal: constValScratch)
	let isConst = try isConstScratch.get(valueId) otherwise false
	if isConst 'recordIt'
		let value = try constValScratch.get(valueId) otherwise 0
		greedyRecordConstant(state, vid: valueId, value: value)
	end 'recordIt'
end 'greedyMaybeRecordConstant'

// Allocate registers for one op and emit its physical form into block.opRefs.
// Order mirrors a standard linear greedy allocator: ensure reads resident ->
// free reads that die here (so a def can reuse a dying source's register) ->
// allocate defs (protecting still-live sources) -> substitute.
function greedyProcessOp(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, opQuery TargetOpQuery, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, packed OpIndex, coloring ColoringResult, lastUse RegIntArray, isConstScratch BoolArray, constValScratch RegIntArray, opIdx RegInt, liveOut LiveSet)
	let reads = RegIntArray.create()
	regTarget.countOpReads(module, reads: reads, packed: packed)
	let defs = RegIntArray.create()
	regTarget.noteOpDefs(module, defs: defs, packed: packed)

	// 1. Ensure every read operand is resident; protect each resolved read from
	// eviction while resolving the remaining reads of the same op.
	var readProtect = 0
	for r in reads 'ensureReads'
		let ord = greedyEnsureInReg(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, vid: r, protectMask: readProtect)
		try coloring.assignment.set(r, value: ord) otherwise panic("greedy: coloring.assignment.set OOB for v{r}")
		readProtect = greedyMaskSet(readProtect, ord: ord)
	end 'ensureReads'

	// 2. Free reads that die at this op so a def can reuse the register. A value
	// that is ALSO defined here (in-place use+def, e.g. addRegImm) keeps its
	// register through the op — it is neither freed nor reallocated. Live-after
	// reads (and in-place defs) are protected so def allocation can't evict them.
	//
	// A value that is LIVE-OUT of this block is never freed here even at its last
	// in-block use: it is needed by a successor and must reach the block-exit
	// boundary spill in a register (or already in its stack home via an eviction).
	// Freeing it with greedyNoteValueDead would clear its register WITHOUT spilling
	// — if it had no stack home yet (a fresh in-block def with no later in-block
	// read), the value would be lost. Keeping it live routes it through the
	// boundary spill instead.
	var liveProtect = 0
	for r in reads 'freeDead'
		let dies = greedyIsLastUse(lastUse, vid: r, opIdx: opIdx) and not liveOut.contains(r)
		let inPlaceDef = idListContains(defs, target: r)
		if dies and not inPlaceDef 'free'
			greedyNoteValueDead(state, vid: r)
		end 'free' else 'keep'
			let ord = getValueInfo(state, valueId: r).gprOrdinal
			liveProtect = greedyMaskSet(liveProtect, ord: ord)
		end 'keep'
	end 'freeDead'

	// 3. Allocate a register for each def. A def already resident (it was a read
	// of this same op — the in-place case) keeps its register.
	for d in defs 'allocDefs'
		let info = getValueInfo(state, valueId: d)
		if info.gprOrdinal != RM_NONE 'alreadyResident'
			try coloring.assignment.set(d, value: info.gprOrdinal) otherwise panic("greedy: coloring.assignment.set OOB for v{d}")
		end 'alreadyResident' else 'fresh'
			let ord = greedyAllocateReg(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, vid: d, protectMask: liveProtect)
			try coloring.assignment.set(d, value: ord) otherwise panic("greedy: coloring.assignment.set OOB for v{d}")
		end 'fresh'

		greedyMaybeRecordConstant(state, opQuery: opQuery, module: module, packed: packed, valueId: d, isConstScratch: isConstScratch, constValScratch: constValScratch)
	end 'allocDefs'

	// 4. Emit the op with the chosen physical registers, reusing the SSA path's
	// per-op substitution (identity moves are elided by it).
	regTarget.substituteOp(module, packed: packed, coloring: coloring, opRefs: block.opRefs)
end 'greedyProcessOp'

// Substitute the terminator (resolving any read operands) and reattach it,
// mirroring applyColoring's terminator handling.
function greedyProcessTerminator(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, opQuery TargetOpQuery, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, coloring ColoringResult)
	let term = block.terminatorIndex()
	greedyGuardOpSupported(state, regTarget: regTarget, opQuery: opQuery, module: module, packed: term)

	let reads = RegIntArray.create()
	regTarget.countOpReads(module, reads: reads, packed: term)
	var readProtect = 0
	for r in reads 'ensureReads'
		let ord = greedyEnsureInReg(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, vid: r, protectMask: readProtect)
		try coloring.assignment.set(r, value: ord) otherwise panic("greedy: coloring.assignment.set OOB for v{r}")
		readProtect = greedyMaskSet(readProtect, ord: ord)
	end 'ensureReads'

	let termOps = OpIndexList.create()
	regTarget.substituteOp(module, packed: term, coloring: coloring, opRefs: termOps)
	if termOps.count() > 0 'hasResult'
		let newTerm = try termOps.get(0) otherwise term
		block.terminator = Terminator.opIndex(newTerm)
	end 'hasResult' else 'noResult'
		// Identity-move elimination dropped the terminator: promote the last
		// regular op (mirrors applyColoring).
		let lastOp = try block.opRefs.removeLast() otherwise 'emptyBlock'
			block.terminator = Terminator.unset
			return
		end 'emptyBlock'
		block.terminator = Terminator.opIndex(lastOp)
	end 'noResult'
end 'greedyProcessTerminator'

// ============================================================
// Call handling (Phase 3): direct calls, GPR-only
// ============================================================

// Walk a block's regular ops with greedy allocation, recognizing CALL regions the
// way ApplyColoring's walkBlockWithCallArgBuffer does — but choosing each move's
// registers greedily instead of reading a precomputed coloring. A call lowers to
// arg-setup movs, the call op, then result-capture movs (see the CALL MODEL note
// at the top of the file). Each region is buffered and handed to the SAME parallel-
// copy sequencer the SSA path uses; the greedy walk just records the registers it
// chose in `coloring.assignment` so the existing classifiers resolve the moves to
// physical ordinals exactly as they do after SSA coloring.
//
// `opIdx` stays aligned with the position in `oldOps` (every op, whatever its role,
// advances it by one) so the last-use scan that drives freeDead / boundary spills
// stays valid.
function greedyWalkBlockOps(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, opQuery TargetOpQuery, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, oldOps OpIndexList, regularOpCount RegInt, coloring ColoringResult, scan GreedyLastUse, isConstScratch BoolArray, constValScratch RegIntArray, liveOut LiveSet)
	var argBuffer = CallArgKindArray.create()
	var argSourceVids = RegIntArray.create()
	var argProtect = 0
	var resultBuffer = CallArgKindArray.create()
	var resultProtect = 0

	var opIdx = 0
	for packed in oldOps 'eachOp'
		if opIdx >= regularOpCount 'doneRegular'
			break
		end 'doneRegular'
		state.currentOpIndex = opIdx

		// Post-call result-capture region: `mov virtual(resultId), physReturnReg`.
		// Detected by the coloring-FREE SHAPE predicate: the capture op IS its dest
		// vreg's defining op, so it is still uncolored here — classifyCallResultCapture
		// would panic resolving the dest ordinal (colorGpr). greedyAllocateCaptureDefs
		// then colors the dest, so the re-classification below resolves the move's
		// dstOrd. Captures accumulate so a value+error-flag pair is sequenced together
		// (the colorer-equivalent greedy choice can place them in a swap a naive
		// two-mov emit would miscompile — see sequentializeCallResultCapture).
		if regTarget.isCallResultCaptureShape(module, packed: packed) 'isCapture'
			resultProtect = greedyAllocateCaptureDefs(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, packed: packed, coloring: coloring, protectMask: resultProtect)
			let kind = regTarget.classifyCallResultCapture(module, packed: packed, coloring: coloring)
			resultBuffer.push(kind)
			opIdx = opIdx + 1
			continue
		end 'isCapture'

		// Any non-capture op ends the capture region: sequence the buffered captures
		// (so the result vregs are materialized and resident) before emitting it.
		if resultBuffer.count() > 0 'flushResults'
			sequentializeCallResultCapture(module, kinds: resultBuffer, dstBlock: block, state: state, regTarget: regTarget)
			resultBuffer.clear()
			resultProtect = 0
		end 'flushResults'

		if opQuery.isCallOp(module, packed: packed) 'isCall'
			greedyProcessCall(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, packed: packed, argBuffer: argBuffer, argSourceVids: argSourceVids, coloring: coloring, lastUse: scan.lastUse, liveOut: liveOut, callOpIdx: opIdx)
			argBuffer.clear()
			argSourceVids.clear()
			argProtect = 0
			opIdx = opIdx + 1
			continue
		end 'isCall'

		// Arg-setup op (`mov physArgReg, virtual(srcId)` / overflow stack store)?
		// Detected by the coloring-FREE SHAPE predicate: the source vreg is colored
		// by greedyPrepareArgSource below, AFTER this probe, so classifyCallArgSetup's
		// eager srcOrd resolve (colorGpr) would panic on an as-yet-uncolored source.
		// Once resident and colored, the re-classification resolves the move's srcOrd
		// and buffers it for the parallel-copy sequencer.
		if regTarget.isCallArgSetupShape(module, packed: packed) 'isArg'
			argProtect = greedyPrepareArgSource(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, packed: packed, coloring: coloring, argSourceVids: argSourceVids, protectMask: argProtect)
			let kind = regTarget.classifyCallArgSetup(module, packed: packed, coloring: coloring)
			argBuffer.push(kind)
			opIdx = opIdx + 1
			continue
		end 'isArg'

		// A non-arg, non-call op while args are buffered ends the arg-setup region
		// WITHOUT a terminating call: DRAIN the buffered moves as a standalone
		// parallel copy, then process this op normally. Mirrors ApplyColoring's
		// walkBlockWithCallArgBuffer routing such an interloper to drainAndSubstitute.
		// The common shape is a function's RETURN-VALUE setup (`mov argReg0, vResult`):
		// arg-reg-0 doubles as the GPR return register (arm64 x0, x64 rax/rcx), so the
		// return-value mov has the exact arg0-setup shape but is followed by the
		// epilogue/ret, not a call — draining emits it as the correct return-value copy.
		// A genuine indirect/witness call (a fn-ptr load interleaved here) still drains
		// its partial args, processes the load, then panics at greedyProcessCall's
		// indirect guard when the call op (which READS the loaded operand) is reached —
		// so that unsupported shape keeps a clear panic.
		if argBuffer.count() > 0 'interloper'
			greedyDrainArgBuffer(module, argBuffer: argBuffer, argSourceVids: argSourceVids, block: block, state: state, regTarget: regTarget)
			argProtect = 0
		end 'interloper'

		greedyGuardOpSupported(state, regTarget: regTarget, opQuery: opQuery, module: module, packed: packed)
		greedyProcessOp(state, regTarget: regTarget, opQuery: opQuery, module: module, block: block, packed: packed, coloring: coloring, lastUse: scan.lastUse, isConstScratch: isConstScratch, constValScratch: constValScratch, opIdx: opIdx, liveOut: liveOut)
		opIdx = opIdx + 1
	end 'eachOp'

	// Block ends mid-region: flush captures (a block can end on the last call's
	// result capture). A buffered arg region with no terminating call is the
	// return-value setup at the end of a block (`mov argReg0, vResult` with the ret in
	// the terminator slot) — DRAIN it as a standalone parallel copy, mirroring
	// ApplyColoring's tail flush, rather than panicking.
	if resultBuffer.count() > 0 'tailResults'
		sequentializeCallResultCapture(module, kinds: resultBuffer, dstBlock: block, state: state, regTarget: regTarget)
	end 'tailResults'
	if argBuffer.count() > 0 'tailArgs'
		greedyDrainArgBuffer(module, argBuffer: argBuffer, argSourceVids: argSourceVids, block: block, state: state, regTarget: regTarget)
	end 'tailArgs'
end 'greedyWalkBlockOps'

// Drain a buffered arg-setup region that is NOT terminated by a call, emitting it as
// a standalone parallel copy through the SAME cycle-safe sequencer the call path uses
// (sequentializeCallArgSetup reads the srcOrd greedyPrepareArgSource recorded in the
// coloring and the fixed-ABI dstOrd). The buffer is then cleared so the interrupting
// op — or the next region — is processed fresh. Mirrors ApplyColoring's
// drainAndSubstitute. No register-file surgery is needed: a copy does not destroy its
// source, so each source value remains valid in the register the walk bound it to.
function greedyDrainArgBuffer(module TargetModule, argBuffer CallArgKindArray, argSourceVids RegIntArray, block IrBlock, state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget)
	sequentializeCallArgSetup(module, kinds: argBuffer, dstBlock: block, state: state, regTarget: regTarget)
	argBuffer.clear()
	argSourceVids.clear()
end 'greedyDrainArgBuffer'

// Ensure every source operand of an arg-setup op is resident, record the chosen
// register in `coloring.assignment` (so classifyCallArgSetup resolves the move's
// srcOrd), protect it from eviction while the rest of the region's sources are
// resolved, and remember the source vid so it can be freed after the call. Returns
// the updated protect mask. Mirrors greedyProcessOp's read-ensure step.
function greedyPrepareArgSource(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, packed OpIndex, coloring ColoringResult, argSourceVids RegIntArray, protectMask RegInt) returns RegInt
	var mask = protectMask
	let reads = RegIntArray.create()
	regTarget.countOpReads(module, reads: reads, packed: packed)
	for r in reads 'eachRead'
		let ord = greedyEnsureInReg(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, vid: r, protectMask: mask)
		try coloring.assignment.set(r, value: ord) otherwise panic("greedy: coloring.assignment.set OOB for arg source v{r}")
		mask = greedyMaskSet(mask, ord: ord)
		argSourceVids.push(r)
	end 'eachRead'
	return mask
end 'greedyPrepareArgSource'

// Allocate a physical register for each virtual DEST of a result-capture op and
// record it in `coloring.assignment` (so classifyCallResultCapture resolves the
// move's dstOrd). The return registers (the capture SOURCES) are already free —
// greedyProcessCall's caller-saved invalidation cleared them — so a dest can even
// land back in its own return register (an identity capture the sequencer drops).
// Protects previously-allocated capture dests from eviction. Returns the updated
// protect mask. Mirrors greedyProcessOp's def-allocation step (a result capture is
// never a constant materialization, so no constant recording is needed).
function greedyAllocateCaptureDefs(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, packed OpIndex, coloring ColoringResult, protectMask RegInt) returns RegInt
	var mask = protectMask
	let defs = RegIntArray.create()
	regTarget.noteOpDefs(module, defs: defs, packed: packed)
	for d in defs 'eachDef'
		let ord = greedyAllocateReg(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, vid: d, protectMask: mask)
		try coloring.assignment.set(d, value: ord) otherwise panic("greedy: coloring.assignment.set OOB for capture dest v{d}")
		mask = greedyMaskSet(mask, ord: ord)
	end 'eachDef'
	return mask
end 'greedyAllocateCaptureDefs'

// Process a CALL op together with its buffered arg-setup region:
//   1. Build the clobber set — caller-saved GPRs plus any callee-saved register
//      this call uses as an arg slot (e.g. x64's rbx 7th-arg) — and spill every
//      live-across resident value there to its stack home so it survives the call
//      (mirrors RegisterManagerBase.SpillAllLiveRegisters). Values dead at the call
//      (consumed args) are skipped.
//   2. Sequence the buffered arg-setup parallel copy into the ABI registers,
//      cycle-safe, reusing sequentializeCallArgSetup with the greedily-chosen
//      sources recorded in `coloring`.
//   3. Emit the (substituted) call op.
//   4. Invalidate the clobber set: those registers no longer hold their pre-call
//      values (spilled to homes above, or dead), so later uses reload (mirrors
//      InvalidateCallerSavedRegisters).
//   5. Free dead arg sources still resident in surviving (callee-saved) registers.
// The post-call result capture is handled by the caller's buffered walk.
function greedyProcessCall(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, packed OpIndex, argBuffer CallArgKindArray, argSourceVids RegIntArray, coloring ColoringResult, lastUse RegIntArray, liveOut LiveSet, callOpIdx RegInt)
	// An indirect/witness call reads its callee (or loaded fn-ptr) through a virtual
	// register; a direct call reads nothing virtual. Pinning that callee operand
	// outside the arg/clobber set is a later phase, so refuse here.
	let callReads = RegIntArray.create()
	regTarget.countOpReads(module, reads: callReads, packed: packed)
	if callReads.count() > 0 'indirect'
		panic("greedy: indirect/witness call in func `{state.funcName}` is not supported by the greedy path yet (only direct calls) — the callee operand would need pinning outside the arg/clobber set")
	end 'indirect'

	let clobberMask = greedyBuildCallClobberMask(state, argBuffer: argBuffer)

	greedySpillAcrossCall(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, clobberMask: clobberMask, lastUse: lastUse, liveOut: liveOut, callOpIdx: callOpIdx)

	sequentializeCallArgSetup(module, kinds: argBuffer, dstBlock: block, state: state, regTarget: regTarget)

	regTarget.substituteOp(module, packed: packed, coloring: coloring, opRefs: block.opRefs)

	greedyInvalidateAcrossCall(state, clobberMask: clobberMask)

	greedyFreeDeadArgSources(state, argSourceVids: argSourceVids, lastUse: lastUse, liveOut: liveOut, callOpIdx: callOpIdx)
end 'greedyProcessCall'

// Build the bitset of GPR ordinals this call destroys: every caller-saved GPR
// (clobbered by the callee) plus every callee-saved GPR used as an arg slot for
// THIS call (clobbered by the arg-setup parallel copy — e.g. x64's rbx 7th-arg
// slot). On targets whose arg registers are all caller-saved (arm64 x0-x7), this
// is exactly the caller-saved set.
function greedyBuildCallClobberMask(state RegState, argBuffer CallArgKindArray) returns RegInt
	var mask = 0
	for ord in state.desc.callerSavedGprs 'callerSaved'
		mask = greedyMaskSet(mask, ord: ord)
	end 'callerSaved'
	for kind in argBuffer 'argDests'
		mask = greedyAddArgDestToMask(mask, kind: kind)
	end 'argDests'
	return mask
end 'greedyBuildCallClobberMask'

// Add a GPR arg-setup move's destination register to the clobber mask. Stack
// writes have no register dest; an FP dest lives in a separate ordinal space the
// GPR clobber mask must not touch (FP is unsupported this phase regardless).
function greedyAddArgDestToMask(mask RegInt, kind CallArgKind) returns RegInt
	return match kind 'k'
		regToReg(_, dstOrd, isFloat) gives greedyMaskAddGprOrd(mask, ord: dstOrd, isFloat: isFloat)
		spillReload(dstOrd, _, isFloat) gives greedyMaskAddGprOrd(mask, ord: dstOrd, isFloat: isFloat)
		stackWrite gives mask
		none gives mask
	end 'k'
end 'greedyAddArgDestToMask'

function greedyMaskAddGprOrd(mask RegInt, ord RegOrdinal, isFloat bool) returns RegInt
	if isFloat 'fp'
		return mask
	end 'fp'
	return greedyMaskSet(mask, ord: ord)
end 'greedyMaskAddGprOrd'

// True iff value `vid` is needed AFTER the call at `callOpIdx`: it is either
// live-out of the block or has an in-block use strictly after the call. Such a
// value must survive the call (spilled to its home); a value dead at the call
// (e.g. a consumed argument) need not be.
function greedyValueLiveAcrossCall(vid RegInt, lastUse RegIntArray, liveOut LiveSet, callOpIdx RegInt) returns bool
	if liveOut.contains(vid) 'liveOut'
		return true
	end 'liveOut'
	return getOr(lastUse, index: vid, fallback: RM_NONE) > callOpIdx
end 'greedyValueLiveAcrossCall'

// Spill every live-across value currently resident in a clobbered register to its
// stack home so it can be reloaded after the call. Constants (rematerializable) and
// values whose home is already in sync are skipped. The register stays OCCUPIED —
// the value's bits are still needed by the arg-setup parallel copy that runs next;
// greedyInvalidateAcrossCall clears the bindings after the call. Mirrors
// RegisterManagerBase.SpillAllLiveRegisters.
function greedySpillAcrossCall(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, clobberMask RegInt, lastUse RegIntArray, liveOut LiveSet, callOpIdx RegInt)
	var ord = 0
	while ord < state.registerContents.count() 'eachReg'
		if greedyMaskHas(clobberMask, ord: ord) 'clobbered'
			let occupant = getOr(state.registerContents, index: ord, fallback: RM_NONE)
			if occupant != RM_NONE 'occupied'
				var info = getValueInfo(state, valueId: occupant)
				let mustSpill = not info.isConstant and not info.homeInSync and greedyValueLiveAcrossCall(occupant, lastUse: lastUse, liveOut: liveOut, callOpIdx: callOpIdx)
				if mustSpill 'needsSpill'
					if info.stackHome == RM_NO_STACK_HOME 'allocHome'
						info.stackHome = greedyAllocateSpillSlot(state)
					end 'allocHome'
					regTarget.emitSpill(module, dstBlock: block, regOrdinal: ord, stackOffset: info.stackHome)
					info.homeInSync = true
				end 'needsSpill'
			end 'occupied'
		end 'clobbered'
		ord = ord + 1
	end 'eachReg'
end 'greedySpillAcrossCall'

// Clear the register-file bindings for every clobbered register after the call:
// they no longer hold their pre-call values (spilled to homes by
// greedySpillAcrossCall, or dead). Subsequent uses reload from the home. Mirrors
// RegisterManagerBase.InvalidateCallerSavedRegisters.
function greedyInvalidateAcrossCall(state RegState, clobberMask RegInt)
	var ord = 0
	while ord < state.registerContents.count() 'eachReg'
		if greedyMaskHas(clobberMask, ord: ord) 'clobbered'
			let occupant = getOr(state.registerContents, index: ord, fallback: RM_NONE)
			if occupant != RM_NONE 'occupied'
				var info = getValueInfo(state, valueId: occupant)
				info.gprOrdinal = RM_NONE
				set(state.registerContents, index: ord, value: RM_NONE, sentinel: RM_NONE)
				set(state.lastUsed, index: ord, value: 0, sentinel: 0)
			end 'occupied'
		end 'clobbered'
		ord = ord + 1
	end 'eachReg'
end 'greedyInvalidateAcrossCall'

// Free any arg-source value dead at the call (its last use was the arg move, and
// it is not live-out) that is still resident — this only matters for a source that
// lived in a surviving callee-saved register, since clobbered registers were
// already invalidated. Keeps the register file tight; correctness does not depend
// on it (a lingering dead value would simply be a future eviction candidate).
function greedyFreeDeadArgSources(state RegState, argSourceVids RegIntArray, lastUse RegIntArray, liveOut LiveSet, callOpIdx RegInt)
	for vid in argSourceVids 'each'
		if not greedyValueLiveAcrossCall(vid, lastUse: lastUse, liveOut: liveOut, callOpIdx: callOpIdx) 'dead'
			greedyNoteValueDead(state, vid: vid)
		end 'dead'
	end 'each'
end 'greedyFreeDeadArgSources'

// ============================================================
// Cross-block support (Phase 2): globals, homes, boundary spills
// ============================================================

// The id of the function's entry block (the first block in layout order).
function greedyEntryBlockId(module TargetModule, func IrFunction) returns BlockId
	let blockRef = try func.blockRefs.get(0) otherwise panic("greedy: func `{func.name}` has no blocks")
	let entry = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("invariant: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
	return entry.id
end 'greedyEntryBlockId'

// blockId -> blockRef (index into module.blocks), built once per function so a
// branch edge's target-block lookup during the boundary spill is O(1).
typealias GreedyBlockIdToRef = Map with (BlockId, BlockRef)

function greedyBuildBlockIdToRef(module TargetModule, func IrFunction) returns GreedyBlockIdToRef
	var map = GreedyBlockIdToRef.create()
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'each'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("invariant: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		map.upsert(block.id, value: blockRef)
	end 'each'
	return map
end 'greedyBuildBlockIdToRef'

// Empty live-out set (for the entry point's per-block lookup fallback and for
// single-block functions whose only block has no successors).
function greedyGetLiveOut(liveness LivenessInfo, blockId BlockId) returns LiveSet
	return try liveness.liveOut.get(blockId) otherwise panic("greedy: liveOut missing for block {blockId} (liveOut sized to numBlocks)")
end 'greedyGetLiveOut'

// Mark value `vid` as GLOBAL: it crosses a block boundary (a block param or a
// value live-out of some block) and therefore needs a stable stack home.
function greedyMarkGlobal(globals BoolArray, vid RegInt)
	if vid < 0 'invalid'
		return
	end 'invalid'
	try globals.set(vid, value: true) otherwise panic("greedy: globals.set OOB for v{vid} (globals sized to maxValueId+1)")
end 'greedyMarkGlobal'

// Compute the set of GLOBAL value ids: every block param (phi dest) plus every
// value in any block's live-out set. These are exactly the values whose lifetime
// crosses a block boundary and that must round-trip through a stable stack home.
function greedyComputeGlobals(module TargetModule, func IrFunction, liveness LivenessInfo, arraySize RegInt) returns BoolArray
	var globals = greedyMakeBoolArray(arraySize)
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("invariant: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachParam'
			greedyMarkGlobal(globals, vid: arg.valueId)
		end 'eachParam'
		let liveOut = greedyGetLiveOut(liveness, blockId: block.id)
		for vid in liveOut.members 'eachLiveOut'
			greedyMarkGlobal(globals, vid: vid)
		end 'eachLiveOut'
	end 'eachBlock'
	return globals
end 'greedyComputeGlobals'

// Assign a stack home to every GLOBAL value UP FRONT, in numeric value-id order
// (deterministic, independent of block processing order). Doing this before any
// block is walked guarantees a reload's home offset is valid even when the
// writer of that home is a block processed later (a loop back-edge).
function greedyPreassignHomes(state RegState, globals BoolArray)
	var vid = 0
	while vid < globals.count() 'eachVid'
		let isGlobal = try globals.get(vid) otherwise false
		if isGlobal 'assign'
			var info = getValueInfo(state, valueId: vid)
			if info.stackHome == RM_NO_STACK_HOME 'noHome'
				info.stackHome = greedyAllocateSpillSlot(state)
			end 'noHome'
		end 'assign'
		vid = vid + 1
	end 'eachVid'
end 'greedyPreassignHomes'

// Empty the register file at a block boundary: the cross-block model assumes NO
// value is resident at block start (every live-in is reloaded from its home).
// Clears the physical-register contents and every value's GPR binding, but
// PRESERVES each value's stackHome / isConstant / constantValue (those persist
// for the whole function so reloads and rematerialization stay consistent).
function greedyResetRegisterFile(state RegState)
	var ord = 0
	while ord < state.registerContents.count() 'clearRegs'
		set(state.registerContents, index: ord, value: RM_NONE, sentinel: RM_NONE)
		set(state.lastUsed, index: ord, value: 0, sentinel: 0)
		ord = ord + 1
	end 'clearRegs'

	var vi = 0
	while vi < state.values.count() 'clearVals'
		var info = try state.values.get(vi) otherwise panic("greedy: values.get OOB at {vi} (vi < count by guard)")
		info.gprOrdinal = RM_NONE
		vi = vi + 1
	end 'clearVals'

	state.nextFreshIndex = 0
end 'greedyResetRegisterFile'

// Seed the register file with the entry block's incoming ABI argument registers.
//
// Function parameters arrive as leading entry-block movRegReg(virtual(vP),
// physical(argReg)) ops. The physical source reports NO read (collectRead drops
// physical operands), so without this seeding the forward walk would think every
// argument register is free and could hand a later value an argument register that
// still holds an as-yet-unconsumed earlier param — clobbering it (a function with
// >= 4 register params would return a wrong argument, since later param movs
// overwrite earlier params' argument registers). Mirroring
// preSeedX64ParamRegisters / preAssignParam, each param vreg is pre-bound to its
// incoming argument register: the param-mov then becomes an identity (vP already
// resides in argReg) and is elided during substitution, and the argument register
// stays occupied — never handed to another value — until the param dies.
//
// Only the LEADING contiguous run of GPR param movs is seeded; the scan stops at
// the first op decodeParamMov does not recognize as a virtual<-physical GPR mov.
// This excludes later physical-source movs (e.g. an idiv result extraction
// `mov vQ, rax`), which always follow compute ops and so are never in the leading
// run, and stops at an FP register param (recorded but unsupported this phase) —
// the forward walk then hits the existing FP-not-supported panic on that FP op.
// Driven entirely by the actual entry ops via the target's decodeParamMov hook, so
// it stays target-neutral and never miscompiles a misrouted function.
function greedySeedEntryParams(state RegState, opQuery TargetOpQuery, module TargetModule, entryBlock IrBlock)
	var i = 0
	let opCount = entryBlock.opRefs.count()
	while i < opCount 'seedLeading'
		let packed = try entryBlock.opRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("greedy: entryBlock.opRefs.get OOB at {i} (i < opCount by guard)")
		let probe = opQuery.decodeParamMov(module, packed: packed)
		if not probe.found 'notParamMov'
			break
		end 'notParamMov'
		greedyAssign(state, ord: probe.srcOrd, vid: probe.destId)
		i = i + 1
	end 'seedLeading'
end 'greedySeedEntryParams'

// Spill every value that is live-OUT of `block` and still resident to its stack
// home, so successors can reload it. Constant values are skipped (the successor
// rematerializes them); values already evicted mid-block are already in their
// home. Homes are pre-assigned for every global, but the RM_NO_STACK_HOME guard
// keeps this self-contained.
function greedyBoundarySpillLiveOut(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, liveOut LiveSet)
	for vid in liveOut.members 'eachLiveOut'
		var info = getValueInfo(state, valueId: vid)
		if info.isConstant 'remat'
			continue
		end 'remat'
		if info.gprOrdinal == RM_NONE 'notResident'
			continue
		end 'notResident'

		if info.stackHome == RM_NO_STACK_HOME 'noHome'
			info.stackHome = greedyAllocateSpillSlot(state)
		end 'noHome'
		regTarget.emitSpill(module, dstBlock: block, regOrdinal: info.gprOrdinal, stackOffset: info.stackHome)
	end 'eachLiveOut'
end 'greedyBoundarySpillLiveOut'

// A resolved block-arg (phi) edge: the physical register holding each arg value
// plus the successor PARAM value id whose stack home receives it (the two arrays
// are parallel). Produced by greedyReadEdgeArgs — which reads the sources into
// registers on the path SHARED by all out-edges (before the terminator) — and
// consumed by greedyEmitEdgeStores, which writes each param home on the edge's
// OWN dynamic path. Splitting read (shared) from store (per-path) is what makes a
// param home that double-serves as a live value AND a phi destination correct:
// the store that overwrites it executes only when its edge is actually taken.
type GreedyEdgePlan
	export var targetBlockId as BlockId
	export var argOrds as RegIntArray
	export var paramValueIds as RegIntArray

	export static function create(targetBlockId BlockId) returns GreedyEdgePlan
		return Self{targetBlockId: targetBlockId, argOrds: RegIntArray.create(), paramValueIds: RegIntArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	export function hasStores() returns bool
		return self.argOrds.count() > 0
	end 'hasStores'
end 'GreedyEdgePlan'

typealias GreedyEdgePlanArray = Array with GreedyEdgePlan

// Read every outgoing edge's block-arg (phi) SOURCE values into registers, BEFORE
// the block's terminator — i.e. on the path shared by all of the block's out-edges.
// Returns one plan per edge recording, per successor-param position, the holding
// ordinal + the param's value id.
//
// Reading on the shared path (rather than on each edge's own path) keeps the two
// dynamic paths' register state identical, so the per-path STORES can reference the
// recorded ordinals without re-deriving register state per path. A reload only
// LOADS a value; it never writes the param home that the store phase targets, so
// hoisting it before the branch cannot corrupt the home-aliasing the store phase
// is being careful about. Args of ALL edges are protected from eviction across the
// whole read so one edge's source register is never reused before its store; within
// an edge every arg is read before any store, so a self-referential phi permutation
// cannot lose a source.
//
// FLAG SAFETY: when this runs at a CONDITIONAL boundary it executes AFTER the
// block's flag-setting compare and BEFORE the appended condJump, so it must not
// clobber the condition flags. Reloads and evictions are flag-neutral on every
// target, but rematerializing a CONSTANT source would, on x64, use the value-0
// `xor reg, reg` zeroing idiom — which CLEARS the flags and corrupts the branch
// condition. The caller passes `flagSafe: true` at conditional boundaries so
// constant sources are materialized through the flag-preserving emit instead
// (arm64's movz is already flag-safe). The plain single-successor boundary has no
// flag-reading terminator, so it leaves `flagSafe` at its default false.
function greedyReadEdgeArgs(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, blockIdToRef GreedyBlockIdToRef, flagSafe bool = false) returns GreedyEdgePlanArray
	var plans = GreedyEdgePlanArray.create()
	var protect = 0
	for edge in block.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
		let succRef = try blockIdToRef.get(edge.targetBlockId) otherwise panic("greedy: branch edge targets blockId {edge.targetBlockId} not in func.blockRefs")
		let succ = try module.blocks.get(succRef) otherwise panic("invariant: blockRef from blockIdToRef not in module.blocks")
		var plan = GreedyEdgePlan.create(edge.targetBlockId)
		if succ.blockArgs.count() == 0 'noArgs'
			plans.push(plan)
			continue
		end 'noArgs'
		if succ.blockArgs.count() != edge.argIds.count() 'argMismatch'
			panic("greedy: edge {block.id}->{edge.targetBlockId} has {edge.argIds.count()} args but successor has {succ.blockArgs.count()} params")
		end 'argMismatch'

		var argIdx = 0
		for param in succ.blockArgs 'readArgs'
			if stdTypeIsFloat(param.argType) 'fpArg'
				panic("greedy: FP block-arg (v{param.valueId}) in func `{state.funcName}` is not supported yet (the FP register file is deferred to a later phase)")
			end 'fpArg'
			let argId = try edge.argIds.get(argIdx) otherwise panic("greedy: edge.argIds.get OOB at {argIdx} (count just checked)")
			let ord = greedyEnsureInReg(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, vid: argId, protectMask: protect, flagSafe: flagSafe)
			protect = greedyMaskSet(protect, ord: ord)
			plan.argOrds.push(ord)
			plan.paramValueIds.push(param.valueId)
			argIdx = argIdx + 1
		end 'readArgs'
		plans.push(plan)
	end 'eachEdge'
	return plans
end 'greedyReadEdgeArgs'

// Store each arg value (already resident in the ordinal greedyReadEdgeArgs recorded)
// into its successor-param's stack home, emitting into `dstBlock` — the predecessor
// itself for the fallthrough edge, a critical-edge trampoline for the taken edge.
// Stores read fixed registers and write distinct (per value id) param homes, so the
// store order is irrelevant and no source register is ever clobbered.
function greedyEmitEdgeStores(plan GreedyEdgePlan, state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, dstBlock IrBlock)
	var i = 0
	while i < plan.argOrds.count() 'eachStore'
		let ord = try plan.argOrds.get(i) otherwise panic("greedy: plan.argOrds.get OOB at {i}")
		let paramId = try plan.paramValueIds.get(i) otherwise panic("greedy: plan.paramValueIds.get OOB at {i}")
		var pinfo = getValueInfo(state, valueId: paramId)
		if pinfo.stackHome == RM_NO_STACK_HOME 'noHome'
			pinfo.stackHome = greedyAllocateSpillSlot(state)
		end 'noHome'
		regTarget.emitSpill(module, dstBlock: dstBlock, regOrdinal: ord, stackOffset: pinfo.stackHome)
		i = i + 1
	end 'eachStore'
end 'greedyEmitEdgeStores'

// The plan for the edge targeting `targetBlockId`. Both the then- and else-targets
// of a conditional branch are outgoing edges of the block, so both are present.
function greedyFindEdgePlan(plans GreedyEdgePlanArray, targetBlockId BlockId) returns GreedyEdgePlan
	for plan in plans 'each'
		if plan.targetBlockId == targetBlockId 'match'
			return plan
		end 'match'
	end 'each'
	panic("greedy: no edge plan for target block {targetBlockId}")
end 'greedyFindEdgePlan'

// Place a conditional branch's per-edge block-arg copies on each edge's OWN dynamic
// path, mirroring destroySSA's critical-edge splitting:
//   * the fallthrough (then) edge's copies go inline AFTER the re-emitted condJump,
//     before the jump to the real then-target;
//   * the taken (else) edge's copies go in a freshly-inserted trampoline block that
//     the condJump is retargeted to and which then jumps to the real else-target.
// The arg sources were already loaded into registers by greedyReadEdgeArgs on the
// shared path, so the stores here only emit register->home writes. Returns the
// updated `nextBlockId` (advanced by one when a trampoline is created). The caller
// has already removed/withheld the condJump from block.opRefs and emitted the
// both-paths boundary spills before it.
function greedyEmitConditionalBoundary(state RegState, regTarget RegAllocTarget, module TargetModule, block IrBlock, blockIdToRef GreedyBlockIdToRef, condBrInfo CondBrInfo, condJumpPacked OpIndex, trampolineRefs BlockRefList, nextBlockId BlockId) returns BlockId
	let elseTarget = condBrInfo.elseTarget
	let thenTarget = pickThenTarget(block, elseTarget: elseTarget)

	// flagSafe: this runs between the block's flag-setting compare and the
	// re-emitted condJump, so a constant source's rematerialization must not
	// clobber the condition flags (see greedyReadEdgeArgs).
	let plans = greedyReadEdgeArgs(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, blockIdToRef: blockIdToRef, flagSafe: true)
	let thenPlan = greedyFindEdgePlan(plans, targetBlockId: thenTarget)
	let elsePlan = greedyFindEdgePlan(plans, targetBlockId: elseTarget)

	// Taken (else) edge: split the critical edge with a trampoline so its copies run
	// ONLY when the branch is taken. Skipped entirely when the else-edge has no args.
	var condTarget = elseTarget
	var newNextBlockId = nextBlockId
	if elsePlan.hasStores() 'elseTrampoline'
		let trampolineId = nextBlockId
		let trampIdx = module.blocks.count()
		var tramp = IrBlock.create(trampolineId, label: "__greedy_phi_tramp_{block.id}_{elseTarget}")
		greedyEmitEdgeStores(elsePlan, state: state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, dstBlock: tramp)
		tramp.terminator = Terminator.opIndex(regTarget.createJmpOp(module, blockId: elseTarget))
		module.blocks.push(tramp)
		trampolineRefs.append(trampIdx)
		condTarget = trampolineId
		newNextBlockId = trampolineId + 1
	end 'elseTrampoline'

	// Re-emit the condJump (retargeted to the trampoline when one was built), then
	// the fallthrough (then) edge copies, then an explicit jump to the real
	// then-target. The condJump carries no register operand, so it needs no
	// coloring substitution — only its branch target may change.
	var condOut = condJumpPacked
	if condTarget != elseTarget 'retarget'
		condOut = regTarget.rewriteCondBrTarget(module, packed: condJumpPacked, newTarget: condTarget)
	end 'retarget'
	block.opRefs.append(condOut)

	if thenPlan.hasStores() 'thenStores'
		greedyEmitEdgeStores(thenPlan, state: state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, dstBlock: block)
	end 'thenStores'

	block.terminator = Terminator.opIndex(regTarget.createJmpOp(module, blockId: thenTarget))
	return newNextBlockId
end 'greedyEmitConditionalBoundary'

// Append every trampoline blockRef to the function's blockRef list. Taking
// `blockRefs` as a List PARAMETER (rather than calling `.append` directly on
// `srcFunc.blockRefs`) keeps the mutation off the IrFunction: a mutating method
// invoked on a struct field would flag the containing function as a mutated
// parameter and force every regalloc caller to thread a mutable function (E3019).
// Mirrors IrModule.createAndRegisterBlock's `blockRefs.append`.
function greedyAppendBlockRefs(blockRefs BlockRefList, trampRefs BlockRefList)
	for trampRef in trampRefs 'each'
		blockRefs.append(trampRef)
	end 'each'
end 'greedyAppendBlockRefs'

// The packed op index of the block's conditional-branch op: the terminator slot
// (Layout 2, condJump promoted by eliminateFallthroughJumps) or the last op of the
// original op list (Layout 1, condJump trailing in opRefs).
function greedyCondJumpPacked(block IrBlock, oldOps OpIndexList, condBrInfo CondBrInfo) returns OpIndex
	if condBrInfo.inTerminator 'inTerm'
		return block.terminatorIndex()
	end 'inTerm'
	return try oldOps.get(oldOps.count() - 1) otherwise panic("greedy: condBr Layout 1 but original op list is empty")
end 'greedyCondJumpPacked'

// ============================================================
// Entry point
// ============================================================

// Greedily allocate `srcFunc` and emit its final physical MIR, processing every
// block in layout order with the cross-block spill-at-boundaries model. Returns
// true on success (the function is fully allocated, same MIR contract as
// applyColoring), or false when the greedy path declines the function, so the
// caller can fall back to the SSA pipeline. The caller is responsible for
// snapshotting/restoring the pristine MIR around this call when it intends to
// fall back.
export function runGreedyAllocation(srcFunc IrFunction, module TargetModule, regTarget RegAllocTarget, desc TargetRegAlloc, opQuery TargetOpQuery) returns bool
	var state = createRegState(desc)
	state.funcName = srcFunc.name

	// Phase 0: CFG + liveness (live-out sets drive the boundary spills).
	let cfg = buildIrFunctionCfg(srcFunc, module: module, opQuery: opQuery)
	let entryBlockId = greedyEntryBlockId(module, func: srcFunc)
	let rpo = computeReversePostorder(entryBlockId, numBlocks: cfg.numBlocks, cfg: cfg)
	let liveness = computeLiveness(module, func: srcFunc, cfg: cfg, rpo: rpo, regTarget: regTarget)

	// liveness.maxValueId is already (highest valueId + 1) — the array size.
	let arraySize = liveness.maxValueId
	var coloring = ColoringResult.create(arraySize)
	let isConstScratch = greedyMakeBoolArray(arraySize)
	let constValScratch = greedyMakeIntArray(arraySize, fill: 0)

	// Pre-assign a stable stack home to every cross-block value.
	let globals = greedyComputeGlobals(module, func: srcFunc, liveness: liveness, arraySize: arraySize)
	greedyPreassignHomes(state, globals: globals)

	let blockIdToRef = greedyBuildBlockIdToRef(module, func: srcFunc)

	// Critical-edge trampolines (taken-edge phi copies) are appended to the function
	// AFTER the block walk so they don't disturb its iteration; nextBlockId is
	// threaded across blocks so each trampoline gets a fresh block id. Mirrors
	// destroySSA's trampoline collection.
	var nextBlockId = srcFunc.nextBlockId
	var trampolineRefs = BlockRefList.create()

	for blockRef in srcFunc.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		var block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("invariant: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		// Per-block last-use must be scanned BEFORE opRefs is replaced.
		let scan = greedyBuildLastUse(module, block: block, regTarget: regTarget)
		let liveOut = greedyGetLiveOut(liveness, blockId: block.id)

		// The conditional-branch shape (if any) must be read off the block BEFORE its
		// op list is rebuilt: a condBr's per-edge copies are placed on their own
		// dynamic path around the re-emitted condJump.
		let condBrInfo = detectCondBrPattern(module, block: block, regTarget: regTarget, opQuery: opQuery)

		// The register file starts empty: every live-in is reloaded from its home.
		greedyResetRegisterFile(state)

		// Entry block only: seed the incoming ABI argument registers from the
		// leading param-movs so each param vreg already resides in its argument
		// register (the param-mov elides) and that register is not handed to a later
		// value before the param is consumed. Runs AFTER the reset (which would
		// otherwise wipe the seed) and BEFORE the op walk.
		if block.id == entryBlockId 'seedEntryParams'
			state.currentOpIndex = 0
			greedySeedEntryParams(state, opQuery: opQuery, module: module, entryBlock: block)
		end 'seedEntryParams'

		// Replace the block's op list; the walk re-emits the physical ops into it.
		let oldOps = block.opRefs
		let boundaryOpIdx = oldOps.count()
		block.opRefs = OpIndexList.create()

		// Process the regular ops. When a condBr's condJump trails in opRefs
		// (Layout 1), withhold it here: it is re-emitted at the boundary so the
		// both-paths boundary spills land BEFORE it and each edge's copies land on
		// their own path. Withholding only the final op preserves the op-index
		// alignment greedyBuildLastUse used for freeDead — the condJump reads
		// nothing, so it contributes no last-use entry to lose.
		var regularOpCount = oldOps.count()
		if condBrInfo.hasCondBr and not condBrInfo.inTerminator 'condJumpInOpRefs'
			regularOpCount = oldOps.count() - 1
		end 'condJumpInOpRefs'

		greedyWalkBlockOps(state, regTarget: regTarget, opQuery: opQuery, module: module, block: block, oldOps: oldOps, regularOpCount: regularOpCount, coloring: coloring, scan: scan, isConstScratch: isConstScratch, constValScratch: constValScratch, liveOut: liveOut)

		// Block-exit boundary (before the terminator): spill live-out values to their
		// homes so successors can reload them. These run on EVERY out-edge, so they
		// must precede the conditional branch. Boundary spills are stores only
		// (constants are skipped), so they are flag-neutral and safe between a
		// flag-setting compare and a flag-reading conditional branch. The subsequent
		// edge-arg source reads CAN rematerialize a constant; greedyEmitConditionalBoundary
		// passes flagSafe so that materialization stays flag-neutral too.
		state.currentOpIndex = boundaryOpIdx
		greedyBoundarySpillLiveOut(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, liveOut: liveOut)

		if condBrInfo.hasCondBr 'condBrBoundary'
			// Conditional branch: place each edge's block-arg copies on that edge's
			// own dynamic path (fallthrough inline, taken edge in a trampoline).
			let condJumpPacked = greedyCondJumpPacked(block, oldOps: oldOps, condBrInfo: condBrInfo)
			greedyGuardOpSupported(state, regTarget: regTarget, opQuery: opQuery, module: module, packed: condJumpPacked)
			nextBlockId = greedyEmitConditionalBoundary(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, blockIdToRef: blockIdToRef, condBrInfo: condBrInfo, condJumpPacked: condJumpPacked, trampolineRefs: trampolineRefs, nextBlockId: nextBlockId)
		end 'condBrBoundary' else 'plainBoundary'
			// Single successor (or none): all block-arg copies run on the one path, so
			// they go inline before the (re-emitted) terminator. A multi-edge block
			// with no detected conditional branch can't have its copies path-separated
			// — that is a malformed/unsupported terminator shape, not a silent case.
			if block.branchEdges.count() > 1 'multiEdgeNoCondBr'
				panic("greedy: block {block.id} in func `{state.funcName}` has {block.branchEdges.count()} outgoing edges but no detected conditional branch — per-edge copies cannot be placed on separate dynamic paths (malformed CFG / unsupported terminator)")
			end 'multiEdgeNoCondBr'

			let plans = greedyReadEdgeArgs(state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, block: block, blockIdToRef: blockIdToRef)
			for plan in plans 'eachPlan'
				greedyEmitEdgeStores(plan, state: state, regTarget: regTarget, module: module, dstBlock: block)
			end 'eachPlan'

			if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
				state.currentOpIndex = boundaryOpIdx
				greedyProcessTerminator(state, regTarget: regTarget, opQuery: opQuery, module: module, block: block, coloring: coloring)
			end 'hasTerm'
		end 'plainBoundary'
	end 'eachBlock'

	// Append the critical-edge trampolines the conditional boundaries inserted, then
	// publish the bumped block-id counter (mirrors destroySSA).
	greedyAppendBlockRefs(srcFunc.blockRefs, trampRefs: trampolineRefs)
	srcFunc.nextBlockId = nextBlockId

	// Reserve frame space for any spill slots used (0 when nothing spilled). The
	// prologue/epilogue pass reads func.spillSize / func.stackSize.
	let spillBytes = totalStackSize(state)
	srcFunc.spillSize = srcFunc.spillSize + spillBytes
	srcFunc.stackSize = srcFunc.stackSize + spillBytes

	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "greedy: func={srcFunc.name} allocated via greedy path (blocks={srcFunc.blockRefs.count()}, spillBytes={spillBytes}, maxValueId={arraySize})")
	return true
end 'runGreedyAllocation'
